{"version":"6.9","hitCount":4,"nextCursorMark":"AoIIPude1ygxNDE1MTM1NA==","nextPageUrl":"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=TITLE%3A%22iodothyronine+selenodeiodinases%22&cursorMark=AoIIPude1ygxNDE1MTM1NA%3D%3D&resultType=core&pageSize=3&format=json","request":{"queryString":"TITLE:\"iodothyronine selenodeiodinases\"","resultType":"core","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":3,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"12586771","source":"MED","pmid":"12586771","doi":"10.1210/en.2002-220960","title":"In vivo dimerization of types 1, 2, and 3 iodothyronine selenodeiodinases.","authorString":"Curcio-Morelli C, Gereben B, Zavacki AM, Kim BW, Huang S, Harney JW, Larsen PR, Bianco AC.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Curcio-Morelli C","lastName":"Curcio-Morelli","firstName":"Cyntia","initials":"C","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Thyroid Section, Division of Endocrinology, Diabetes and Hypertension, Brigham and Women's Hospital and Harvard Medical School, Boston, Massachusetts 02115, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Gereben B","lastName":"Gereben","firstName":"Balazs","initials":"B"},{"fullName":"Zavacki AM","lastName":"Zavacki","firstName":"Ann Marie","initials":"AM"},{"fullName":"Kim BW","lastName":"Kim","firstName":"Brian W","initials":"BW"},{"fullName":"Huang S","lastName":"Huang","firstName":"Stephen","initials":"S"},{"fullName":"Harney JW","lastName":"Harney","firstName":"John W","initials":"JW"},{"fullName":"Larsen PR","lastName":"Larsen","firstName":"P Reed","initials":"PR"},{"fullName":"Bianco AC","lastName":"Bianco","firstName":"Antonio C","initials":"AC","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7737-6813"}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7737-6813"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","related_data","wikipedia"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"3","volume":"144","journalIssueId":1169540,"dateOfPublication":"2003 Mar","monthOfPublication":3,"yearOfPublication":2003,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Endocrinology","title":"Endocrinology","essn":"1945-7170","issn":"0013-7227","isoabbreviation":"Endocrinology","nlmid":"0375040"},"printPublicationDate":"2003-03-01"},"pubYear":"2003","pageInfo":"937-946","abstractText":"The goal of the present investigation was to test the hypothesis that types 1, 2, and 3 iodothyronine selenodeiodinases (D1, D2, and D3) can form homodimers. The strategy included transient coexpression of wild-type (wt) deiodinases (target), and FLAG-tagged alanine or cysteine mutants (bait) in human embryonic kidney epithelial cells. SDS-PAGE of the immunoprecipitation pellet of (75)Se-labeled cell lysates using anti-FLAG antibody revealed bands of the correct sizes for the respective wt enzymes, which corresponded to approximately 2-5% of the total deiodinase protein in the cell lysate. Western blot analysis with anti-FLAG antibody of lysates of cells transiently expressing individual FLAG-tagged-cysteine deiodinases revealed specific monomeric bands for each deiodinase and additional minor bands of relative molecular mass (M(r)) of 55,000 for D1, M(r) 62,000 for D2, and M(r) 65,000 for D3, which were eliminated by 100 mM dithiothreitol at 100 C. Anti-FLAG antibody immunodepleted 10% of D1 and 38% of D2 activity from lysates of cells coexpressing inactive FLAG-tagged Ala mutants and the respective wt enzymes (D1 or D2) but failed to immunodeplete wtD3 activity. D1 or D2 activities were present in these respective pellets. We conclude 1) that overexpressed selenodeiodinases can homodimerize probably through disulfide bridges; and 2) at least for D1 and D2, monomeric forms are catalytically active, demonstrating that only one wt monomer partner is required for catalytic activity of these two deiodinases.","affiliation":"Thyroid Section, Division of Endocrinology, Diabetes and Hypertension, Brigham and Women's Hospital and Harvard Medical School, Boston, Massachusetts 02115, USA.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, U.S. Gov't, P.H.S.","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"NIDDK NIH HHS","acronym":"DK","grantId":"DK-36256","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cell Line"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Disulfides","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CH","qualifierName":"chemistry"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Selenium Radioisotopes"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Dithiothreitol","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PD","qualifierName":"pharmacology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Iodide Peroxidase","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"CH","qualifierName":"chemistry"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Recombinant Proteins","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CH","qualifierName":"chemistry"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Blotting, Western"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Immunosorbent Techniques"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Electrophoresis, Polyacrylamide Gel"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Transfection"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Mutagenesis, Site-Directed"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Isotope Labeling"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Gene Expression"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Structure-Activity Relationship"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Dimerization"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Mutation"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Catalysis"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Molecular Weight"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Iodothyronine Deiodinase Type II"}]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"iodothyronine deiodinase type II","registryNumber":"EC 1.11.1.-"},{"name":"iodothyronine deiodinase type I","registryNumber":"EC 1.11.1.-"},{"name":"iodothyronine deiodinase type III","registryNumber":"EC 1.11.1.-"},{"name":"Disulfides","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Iodide Peroxidase","registryNumber":"EC 1.11.1.8"},{"name":"Recombinant Proteins","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Selenium Radioisotopes","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Dithiothreitol","registryNumber":"T8ID5YZU6Y"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1210/en.2002-220960"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":58,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"Y","dbCrossReferenceList":{"dbName":["UNIPROT"]},"hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasData":"Y","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2003-03-28","dateOfCreation":"2003-02-15","dateOfRevision":"2026-01-28","firstPublicationDate":"2003-03-01","firstIndexDate":"2008-01-29","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"11844744","source":"MED","pmid":"11844744","doi":"10.1210/edrv.23.1.0455","title":"Biochemistry, cellular and molecular biology, and physiological roles of the iodothyronine selenodeiodinases.","authorString":"Bianco AC, Salvatore D, Gereben B, Berry MJ, Larsen PR.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Bianco AC","lastName":"Bianco","firstName":"Antonio C","initials":"AC","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7737-6813"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Thyroid Division, Department of Medicine, Brigham and Women's Hospital and Harvard Medical School, Boston, Massachusetts 02115, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Salvatore D","lastName":"Salvatore","firstName":"Domenico","initials":"D","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4556-7620"}},{"fullName":"Gereben B","lastName":"Gereben","firstName":"Balázs","initials":"B"},{"fullName":"Berry MJ","lastName":"Berry","firstName":"Marla J","initials":"MJ"},{"fullName":"Larsen PR","lastName":"Larsen","firstName":"P Reed","initials":"PR"}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7737-6813"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4556-7620"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","wikipedia"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"23","journalIssueId":1052664,"dateOfPublication":"2002 Feb","monthOfPublication":2,"yearOfPublication":2002,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Endocr Rev","title":"Endocrine reviews","essn":"1945-7189","issn":"0163-769X","isoabbreviation":"Endocr Rev","nlmid":"8006258"},"printPublicationDate":"2002-02-01"},"pubYear":"2002","pageInfo":"38-89","abstractText":"The goal of this review is to place the exciting advances that have occurred in our understanding of the molecular biology of the types 1, 2, and 3 (D1, D2, and D3, respectively) iodothyronine deiodinases into a biochemical and physiological context. We review new data regarding the mechanism of selenoprotein synthesis, the molecular and cellular biological properties of the individual deiodinases, including gene structure, mRNA and protein characteristics, tissue distribution, subcellular localization and topology, enzymatic properties, structure-activity relationships, and regulation of synthesis, inactivation, and degradation. These provide the background for a discussion of their role in thyroid physiology in humans and other vertebrates, including evidence that D2 plays a significant role in human plasma T(3) production. We discuss the pathological role of D3 overexpression causing \"consumptive hypothyroidism\" as well as our current understanding of the pathophysiology of iodothyronine deiodination during illness and amiodarone therapy. Finally, we review the new insights from analysis of mice with targeted disruption of the Dio2 gene and overexpression of D2 in the myocardium.","affiliation":"Thyroid Division, Department of Medicine, Brigham and Women's Hospital and Harvard Medical School, Boston, Massachusetts 02115, USA.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, U.S. Gov't, P.H.S.","Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","Review","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"NIDDK NIH HHS","acronym":"DK","grantId":"DK-07529","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIDDK NIH HHS","acronym":"DK","grantId":"DK-44128","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIDDK NIH HHS","acronym":"DK","grantId":"DK-58538","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIDDK NIH HHS","acronym":"DK","grantId":"DK-36256","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Iodide Peroxidase","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"PH","qualifierName":"physiology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CH","qualifierName":"chemistry"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Proteins"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Protein Biosynthesis"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Amino Acid Sequence","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Base Sequence","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Sequence Homology, Amino Acid"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Polymorphism, Genetic","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PH","qualifierName":"physiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Molecular Sequence Data"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Selenoproteins"}]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"selenoproteins","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Proteins","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Iodide Peroxidase","registryNumber":"EC 1.11.1.8"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Unpaywall","url":"https://academic.oup.com/edrv/article-pdf/23/1/38/8860351/edrv0038.pdf"},{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"endojournals.org","url":"https://doi.org/10.1210/er.23.1.38"},{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1210/edrv.23.1.0455"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":1199,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2002-04-23","dateOfCreation":"2002-02-15","dateOfRevision":"2022-03-31","firstPublicationDate":"2002-02-01","firstIndexDate":"2008-01-29","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"12847093","source":"MED","pmid":"12847093","doi":"10.1074/jbc.m305725200","title":"The iodothyronine selenodeiodinases are thioredoxin-fold family proteins containing a glycoside hydrolase clan GH-A-like structure.","authorString":"Callebaut I, Curcio-Morelli C, Mornon JP, Gereben B, Buettner C, Huang S, Castro B, Fonseca TL, Harney JW, Larsen PR, Bianco AC.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Callebaut I","lastName":"Callebaut","firstName":"Isabelle","initials":"I","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3124-887X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Poôle Bio, Laboratoive de Minéralogie-Cristallographie de Paris, CNRS UMR7590, Universities Paris 6 and Paris 7, Paris 75252 Cedex 05, France."}]}},{"fullName":"Curcio-Morelli C","lastName":"Curcio-Morelli","firstName":"Cyntia","initials":"C"},{"fullName":"Mornon JP","lastName":"Mornon","firstName":"Jean-P","initials":"JP"},{"fullName":"Gereben B","lastName":"Gereben","firstName":"Balazs","initials":"B"},{"fullName":"Buettner C","lastName":"Buettner","firstName":"Christoph","initials":"C"},{"fullName":"Huang S","lastName":"Huang","firstName":"Stephen","initials":"S"},{"fullName":"Castro B","lastName":"Castro","firstName":"Bertrand","initials":"B"},{"fullName":"Fonseca TL","lastName":"Fonseca","firstName":"Tatiana L","initials":"TL"},{"fullName":"Harney JW","lastName":"Harney","firstName":"John W","initials":"JW"},{"fullName":"Larsen PR","lastName":"Larsen","firstName":"P Reed","initials":"PR"},{"fullName":"Bianco AC","lastName":"Bianco","firstName":"Antonio C","initials":"AC","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7737-6813"}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7737-6813"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3124-887X"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","recommended_reviews","related_data","wikipedia"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"38","volume":"278","journalIssueId":1153745,"dateOfPublication":"2003 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2003,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"J Biol Chem","title":"The Journal of biological chemistry","essn":"1083-351X","issn":"0021-9258","isoabbreviation":"J Biol Chem","nlmid":"2985121R"},"printPublicationDate":"2003-09-01"},"pubYear":"2003","pageInfo":"36887-36896","abstractText":"The three iodothyronine selenodeiodinases catalyze the initiation and termination of thyroid hormone effects in vertebrates. Structural analyses of these proteins have been hindered by their integral membrane nature and the inefficient eukaryotic-specific pathway for selenoprotein synthesis. Hydrophobic cluster analysis used in combination with Position-specific Iterated BLAST reveals that their extramembrane portion belongs to the thioredoxin-fold superfamily for which experimental structure information exists. Moreover, a large deiodinase region imbedded in the thioredoxin fold shares strong similarities with the active site of iduronidase, a member of the clan GH-A-fold of glycoside hydrolases. This model can explain a number of results from previous mutagenesis analyses and permits new verifiable insights into the structural and functional properties of these enzymes.","affiliation":"Poôle Bio, Laboratoive de Minéralogie-Cristallographie de Paris, CNRS UMR7590, Universities Paris 6 and Paris 7, Paris 75252 Cedex 05, France.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, U.S. Gov't, P.H.S.","Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"NIDDK NIH HHS","acronym":"DK","grantId":"DK58538","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIDDK NIH HHS","acronym":"DK","grantId":"DK 36256","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cell Line"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Thyroid Hormones","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Glycoside Hydrolases","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CH","qualifierName":"chemistry"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Iodide Peroxidase","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"PH","qualifierName":"physiology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"CH","qualifierName":"chemistry"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Bacterial Proteins","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CH","qualifierName":"chemistry"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName"